ing unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=4:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "45807d9e-70fa-58c2-a3d7-d8cc5cbfdcc9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519649}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519632 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492837 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c4c2ff5-edba-514a-87a6-58daf1e367d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492271} {:answer/id #uuid "5b0214e5-26b9-58f4-866b-8bc611fb2ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492397} {:answer/id #uuid "d70b4dc0-8536-581a-8bd5-450e119abdcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492642} {:answer/id #uuid "eb3d2221-dc50-598c-ba5a-1cb5379b5184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492672} {:answer/id #uuid "f99577b0-7bae-5456-97fc-603ae4a89241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492696}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492642} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4ec5a8e-4792-5032-a904-759d0e616ab6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1e463920-832a-54ce-b343-95505e40d97a" :question/math-academy-id "q-170527" :question/problem "The minimum value of $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-12{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-9{x}_{2}^{2},$ when ${x}_{1}^{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}=9,$ is $-18.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=3$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{3}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}18 & -18 \\\\ -18 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we minimize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the minimum value of the quadratic form is $-18,$ we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{min}=-18.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-18:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-18)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}18 & -18 \\\\ -18 & -9\\end{bmatrix}+18\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}36 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-(-18)I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{min}} & =P{u}_{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "491341df-0c55-56c0-a053-5047b84d6caf"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519649 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198685 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "964ff685-0c8f-5399-a6db-e7e9ff284b45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194742} {:answer/id #uuid "c4372638-eac2-5651-a42e-b474aa8e5014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194743} {:answer/id #uuid "e585951e-1933-5d5a-a55e-f897fdd4664a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194744} {:answer/id #uuid "135fadcd-1711-57c3-92f2-65d7240f6b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194745} {:answer/id #uuid "5642d232-0433-5476-bf0e-5697fbc0a389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-140" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194746}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186194746} :answer-field/id #uuid "223a3270-62e6-5297-b31c-f1bd2215a506" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8a89d201-6c7f-5b6a-9c34-e445b93c0f7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-170160" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=5{x}_{1}^{2}-16{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $25{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=100.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -8 \\\\ -8 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=5$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{5},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & -8 \\\\ -8 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -16 \\\\ -40 & -20\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & -80 \\\\ -80 & -100\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}20-\\lambda  & -80 \\\\ -80 & -100-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (20-\\lambda )(-100-\\lambda )-6\\,400 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+80\\lambda -8\\,400 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -60)(\\lambda +140) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =60,\\,-140\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-140.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8d69128b-9c71-5aba-b4db-fd173418e1a2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d9e2ab8-f0d5-5abe-b74e-db6a46609bb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492276} {:answer/id #uuid "208b060c-d7f6-5417-9e03-796577024e7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492282} {:answer/id #uuid "96521b38-e0b7-58e6-b1db-eeedf6a34c29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492507} {:answer/id #uuid "c0497484-5b80-5f14-9367-302334efa418" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492600} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b05c42-2662-52e1-be4f-95016554eeb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492697}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492600} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd63ecac-8751-5520-937a-d3c900eac80e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1c7906bc-d806-5034-89ba-962181be4d73" :question/math-academy-id "q-170402" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $0.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe  maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$  \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a61dcaf7-f154-5c88-a2cc-4dd236b50f6e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519632}}] :activity/title "Constrained Optimization of Quadratic Forms: Determining Where Extrema are Attained" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518976}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199307} :activity/id #uuid "31aab6dd-2cc2-5648-88d8-e57c688c618a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049016 :topic/id #uuid "d40c7f73-0923-5b33-8a87-c53afe20cdeb" :topic/math-academy-id 4158} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199271 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198371 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198013 :question/id #uuid "c8dd9065-9db8-53da-b6a5-cf6cddd3a3e6" :question/math-academy-id "e-11675"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c38eccf8-9941-58de-888c-c44130ed48da" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050605}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281638}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Repeated Integral as a Mixed Integral"} :step/id #uuid "2ecadc5e-e56f-5cb0-8a78-a7a91da258bd" :step/math-academy-id 43193 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199272}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199272 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198372 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198014 :question/id #uuid "83df52ed-139b-5dff-8b32-942aafeff0ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-11676"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e87509d-95fe-5804-a94b-d42ada81e4f7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389312}] :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration in a Triple Integral"} :step/id #uuid "08180a9a-2473-5b5c-bf97-d2ef2156c578" :step/math-academy-id 43194 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199310}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199307 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199474 :tutorial/content "Consider the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\int_0^1 \\int_0^{1-x} \\int_0^{1-x-y} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFor this repeated integral, the integration domain is the type I region\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1-x, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1-x-y \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/56/56ef205e4a92cf96d2ddb81635b5776ededf880d4507e68db1d66ae045533705.png)\n\nSuppose we wish to change the order of integration so that we integrate first with respect to $y,$ then over a region $D$ in the $xz$-plane. In other words, we wish to express our repeated integral in the form\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1 \\int_0^{1-x} \\int_0^{1-x-y} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left[ \\int_{u_1(x,z)}^{u_2(x,z)} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y \\right] \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x$ to $\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}A,$ we write $R$ as a type III region. \n\nTo visualize $R$ as a type III region, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/3c/3c444ce37b1799e950b52789d7837b699938bec602e274fed7c91640a31812a2.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the back boundary and leaves through the front boundary:\n\n- The front boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz = 1-x-y,\n$$\n\nand writing this in the form $y = u_2(x,z),$ we have\n\n$$\ny = 1-x-z.\n$$\n\n- The back boundary of the region is $y = 0.$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is \n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: (x,z) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1-x-z \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/31/31c74e0c21a02898e584d141e942037cedf392680b9bc3bd84ae8831fb55224c.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $y$ and then over $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x-y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x-z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b266db14-83cd-503a-9a9a-0a046db8b914" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7667 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4ef19e30-5711-5c99-9378-bdff12e2c640" :step/math-academy-id 44543 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199271}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199310 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198396 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198038 :question/id #uuid "15684778-4ac6-5741-b17b-4ba9f8a193e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12405"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1eaf02c-e29b-58dd-aa24-8b0bb70183a0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366104}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral by Swapping the Order of Integration"} :step/id #uuid "bdf0c9c2-cbc5-5ca4-8248-c9b49ffdbda4" :step/math-academy-id 44564}] :activity/title "Changing the Order of Integration in Triple Integrals: Changing Region" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198491}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163036} :activity/id #uuid "fcbebdc5-6295-5f6d-84c8-dd3b196003b8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049282 :topic/id #uuid "e9af0219-7eb8-5b10-84c1-b7b5e8e30ae2" :topic/math-academy-id 3135} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163036 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163308 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the least-squares problem $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where \n\n![](images/4d/4d538e6a37eccc1f49f34d3aa1b31a0539d50b351e6029595af1b1f7e0f2967a.svg)\n\nRecall that the corresponding least-squares solution is a vector ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\Vert  A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nIn real-world problems, the coefficients stored in $A$ might come from sources that are not equally reliable. For example, the coefficients might be collected from population surveys with different sample sizes. As a result, we might want to assign a greater \"weight\" to the more reliable data.\n\nFor example, suppose we require the weight of the coefficients in the first row to be twice the weights of the other rows. Then, we can re-state our problem by saying that, for the matrix of weights $W,$ given by\n\n![](images/72/7268fdd7c8701a9cf780342d6eab11015d7764694d17c28a2fae2336db267223.svg)\n\nwe wish to find vectors ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\Vert  W (A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b}) \\Vert\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nTherefore, instead of solving $A\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{b},$ we now need to solve the least-squares problem \n\n$$\nW\\!A\\mathbf x = W\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nTo get the normal equation corresponding to this least-squares problem, we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\!A)^T{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n![](images/0c/0c7459270d9d27c40bdf5b0e6dc7eb0c7e17951541b77936804a9057f488c13f.svg)\n\nthe normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}36 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}9 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}72 \\\\ -54\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the final equation, we obtain the least-squares solution ![](images/72/7259d4c16fa297be7b02cab3ed48a848d1fa73de61c2f14351885072c93c6362.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "92c4b629-686d-5377-b646-ffe0dd0d636a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6076 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "163538b7-47fd-5723-b154-a2b53db447fa" :step/math-academy-id 36972 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163037}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163037 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162092 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161905 :question/id #uuid "2acc65a4-62df-5e61-a3c9-cb8a08e1ff8c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9831"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af51b0b6-4ae7-5b6a-b151-7f8cb88fa186" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048284}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518129}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Normal Equation for a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"} :step/id #uuid "285c2b46-e74a-5ef0-8ec1-c86989af141c" :step/math-academy-id 36973 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163038}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163038 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162093 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161906 :question/id #uuid "22ff2997-c3c3-560e-afdc-52da342dcc10" :question/math-academy-id "e-9832"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b21c27bd-5c7a-5645-93b5-0af73c59f685" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049557}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443607}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution to a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"} :step/id #uuid "7800afe1-dfc0-5278-8b64-b02ebb92f823" :step/math-academy-id 36975 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163045}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163045 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163312 :tutorial/content "Recall that if the solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the least-squares problem $A\\mathbf x=\\mathbf{b}$ is unique, then $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ lies in $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ Furthermore, $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ is the vector in $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ that's closest to $\\mathbf b.$ \n\nIn terms of vector spaces, the vector $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ is the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{b}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of $A.$ Therefore, the error vector $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b}$ is orthogonal to $\\textrm{Col}(A).$\n\n![](images/3a/3a1c79036954f0e04212892b7e3d79c75f8cd7b81d7785441f090f89a4eb6faf.png)\n\nA similar property holds for weighted least-squares solutions:\n\n*If the solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the least-squares problem $W\\!A\\mathbf x=W\\mathbf{b}$ is unique, then\n$A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b}$ is orthogonal to $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ with respect to the weighted dot product defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle_W = \\sum_{i=1}^n w_i^2 x_i y_i,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the $w_i$'s are the weights from the main diagonal of the weight matrix $W.$* \n\nWe previously considered the weighted least-squares problem $W\\!Ax=W\\mathbf{b},$ where\n\n![](images/d4/d44c48deb0864fb8fd6aa92ce8af408b600de8abf2039a3f3cd7397fabc7d9fe.svg)\n\nIt's easy to show (using the usual techniques) that the unique weighted least-squares solution is given by \n\n![](images/db/db8f62fcb085d8c1fcfc22bb53cb8f22bd9a65b211e5fd5d4017e30bf9bdb4c7.svg)\n\nComputing our error vector $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon} = A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ε & =A\\hat{x}-b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $\\mathbf a_1$ and $\\mathbf a_2$ denote the columns of $A\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](images/3f/3fbafcf3f9d80d2d54089647b2f0239662dcbe56c9ba68539e2303ce752d28ee.svg)\n\nNotice that the error vector $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$ is *not* orthogonal to the columns of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- Computing $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon} \\cdot \\mathbf a_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ε\\cdot {a}_{1} & =⟨\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =(1)(1)+(4)(1)+(-4)(2) \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon} \\cdot \\mathbf a_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ε\\cdot {a}_{2} & =⟨\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =(1)(-1)+(4)(0)+(-4)(-1) \\\\  & =3 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we check the orthogonality with respect to the *weighted* dot product \n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle_W = \\sum_{i=1}^n w_i^2 x_i y_i = 4x_1y_1 + x_2y_2 + x_3y_3,\n\\quad\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⟨ε,{a}_{1}⟩}_{W} & ={⟨\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}⟩}_{W} \\\\  & =4(1)(1)+(4)(1)+(-4)(2) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {⟨ε,{a}_{2}⟩}_{W} & ={⟨\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}⟩}_{W} \\\\  & =4(1)(-1)+(4)(0)+(-4)(-1) \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b}$ is orthogonal to $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ with respect to the weighted dot product. We can write this as\n\n$$\nA{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\perp_W \\textrm{Col}(A).\n$$\n\nIn terms of vector spaces, the vector $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ is the orthogonal projection with respect to $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle_W$ of the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of $A.$ \n\nFurthermore, the weighted least-squares problem with weight matrix $W$ is equivalent to finding a vector ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\Vert  A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert_W\n$$\n\nis as small as possible." :tutorial/id #uuid "91e0f414-7785-5748-bba9-04278c6519ad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6084 :tutorial/title "A Geometric Interpretation of the Weighted Least-Squares Solution"} :step/id #uuid "373b333f-64bb-5518-823a-a53a7c7b2614" :step/math-academy-id 37006}] :activity/title "Weighted Least-Squares" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162229}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412663} :activity/id #uuid "5ae33ea6-f725-5358-bc56-131c7aa58050" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049282 :topic/id #uuid "e9af0219-7eb8-5b10-84c1-b7b5e8e30ae2" :topic/math-academy-id 3135} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412660 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "088981d3-114e-574b-937a-924f96c1a577" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412634} {:answer/id #uuid "495b45ed-6e05-5844-806a-6ba5b784755f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412639} {:answer/id #uuid "552d678b-530b-5d1a-bf8b-91ab4eb12691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412640} {:answer/id #uuid "e4052908-8f95-52dd-aefc-cb82ac135212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412649} {:answer/id #uuid "fb119cac-1cdf-5bd7-b4cf-2acfdf9b0565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412653}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412634} :answer-field/id #uuid "fea215fb-04f0-534e-86ff-be0201f49602" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f6bb363c-48b6-5047-89db-8e3fcc537bc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-137102" :question/problem "Find the solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,W=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=\\begin{bmatrix}{2}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}12 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the matrix on the left-hand side is invertible, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12\\cdot 5-(-6)\\cdot (-6)}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\begin{bmatrix}40 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "41c0539c-002c-5259-832d-9bb45ac8b928" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412662}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412661 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412658 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "466cb1d5-bbaa-5bcf-a4b9-8e35b9769bc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412638} {:answer/id #uuid "7390aa1b-1e0b-5b8f-8041-bba7a08ed6ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412642} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d7ad48-ee12-53ed-ae80-3bfaa814df4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412647} {:answer/id #uuid "cb0cdbb1-4af3-53da-b876-22b6640991ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412648} {:answer/id #uuid "f47c99c4-37e6-574e-a7cb-b1c519e96e66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412650}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412642} :answer-field/id #uuid "68d36053-187e-5834-b46e-6140de6fde31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7e019bd1-c120-5401-ab70-85681df29503" :question/math-academy-id "q-137930" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-22 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ shown above. If the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-22 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ -4 & -12 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-22 \\\\ 44 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 27 \\\\ 27 & 81\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-81 \\\\ -243\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 27 & -81 \\\\ 27 & 81 & -243\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 27 & -81 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-9-3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-9-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+\\frac{b}{c}=-9+\\frac{(-3)}{1}=-12.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4cdf2d74-0bb9-5a46-a586-06014aa796e7"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412662 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412659 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "598cbe9b-f2eb-52d5-9e41-b48441ed64d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412641} {:answer/id #uuid "84e93a6b-0108-570f-8119-23d513088357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412643} {:answer/id #uuid "8e9cc8d3-0106-5ea8-86d6-66b4ea64a0cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412644} {:answer/id #uuid "934431cc-66ef-56d1-a66f-d360d9139b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412645} {:answer/id #uuid "f9cbe89f-eecc-5c0e-b93a-5822e57acd6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412651}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412641} :answer-field/id #uuid "b982590b-c792-58f1-b493-14196b43933c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8d91806b-81b0-566e-90e0-48dc8925cff9" :question/math-academy-id "q-137886" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & 20 \\\\ 20 & b\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}10 & 20 \\\\ 20 & 40\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=10+40+6=56.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "887b3834-be22-5f6c-8700-a163d7c04bba" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412664}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412663 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162349 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f5c53a9-a2e1-5c97-ae8a-eca40c100135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412635} {:answer/id #uuid "2c6fec5d-06d4-5c30-a152-268334590e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412636} {:answer/id #uuid "465218a2-69bf-5497-8469-e2312eb7efd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412637} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff085af-9e73-5865-91af-322d3998891a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412646} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9d5a16-3904-5615-8861-702cbf9dba03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "59" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412652}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412652} :answer-field/id #uuid "0676c358-31ac-5dc1-99e9-f46cd0df84d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "363b8573-3582-5360-9547-0ffac7abdb28" :question/math-academy-id "q-137101" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,W=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\,A=Wb$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & 18 \\\\ 18 & b\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=\\begin{bmatrix}{2}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}24 & 18 \\\\ 18 & 17\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=24+17+18=59.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9b0b78c1-38dc-5b63-a705-26a38e682db9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412660}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412664 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411867 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "118a9ee1-aa44-5777-9118-5227ee29fe39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411690} {:answer/id #uuid "343f47c9-bb05-5183-9f14-e8b4fa694501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411704} {:answer/id #uuid "4d9f0f42-a4f1-5465-a3a2-7a60cda35ef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411719} {:answer/id #uuid "8cb4a560-b34c-5ecb-b912-d45e89d2bc34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411753} {:answer/id #uuid "fd063dda-be76-5f0e-93fe-6561f604b438" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411817}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411719} :answer-field/id #uuid "4272bc52-3700-5a4c-920e-c054cf89452b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9a9c63b9-55aa-5f88-8ff0-b14dd8c3b850" :question/math-academy-id "q-137889" :question/problem "Find the solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}17 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the matrix on the left-hand side is invertible, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}17 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 17\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{81}\\begin{bmatrix}-81 \\\\ 81\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bf465e2e-a5d2-5c64-9820-18efd555ee8e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412661}}] :activity/title "Weighted Least-Squares" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412633}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443610} :activity/id #uuid "f2ca585c-f31d-5154-8e11-9d2a532a2dd5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049282 :topic/id #uuid "e9af0219-7eb8-5b10-84c1-b7b5e8e30ae2" :topic/math-academy-id 3135} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443608 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443607 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10b1479a-f401-5be2-8d54-998ace998ee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443601} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb217f8-2e73-5566-a846-de9689a469fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443602} {:answer/id #uuid "95c93c03-292b-584d-aa3a-7dff77290397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443603} {:answer/id #uuid "bc30efa1-ce98-5d2b-b5f5-b28ca6d26bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443604} {:answer/id #uuid "df31033e-dd1d-5f18-81f3-78e9e429df55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443605}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443602} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc00c000-8569-5a9b-b4f0-7026f8c3241d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "642db6cf-d41e-5a55-a66e-30cab01320f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-137924" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ shown above. If the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $2a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -8 & 16 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}18 & -36 \\\\ -36 & 72\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}27 \\\\ -54\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}18 & -36 & 27 \\\\ -36 & 72 & -54\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}18 & -36 & 27 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+\\frac{b}{c}=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{2}{1}=5.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2743865c-4e4f-50db-8594-c5aadb354d23" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443611}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443609 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412659 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "598cbe9b-f2eb-52d5-9e41-b48441ed64d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412641} {:answer/id #uuid "84e93a6b-0108-570f-8119-23d513088357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412643} {:answer/id #uuid "8e9cc8d3-0106-5ea8-86d6-66b4ea64a0cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412644} {:answer/id #uuid "934431cc-66ef-56d1-a66f-d360d9139b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412645} {:answer/id #uuid "f9cbe89f-eecc-5c0e-b93a-5822e57acd6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412651}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412641} :answer-field/id #uuid "b982590b-c792-58f1-b493-14196b43933c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8d91806b-81b0-566e-90e0-48dc8925cff9" :question/math-academy-id "q-137886" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & 20 \\\\ 20 & b\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}10 & 20 \\\\ 20 & 40\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=10+40+6=56.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "68aa0dad-4cc3-5471-b600-86fdb166ad94" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443608}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443610 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8226456a-01f0-5577-ae2a-e36c51dc2d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159309} {:answer/id #uuid "97023f76-2165-5875-884b-00124cfc7eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159310} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6021b0-6cc8-5e87-80d1-4225584902b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159311} {:answer/id #uuid "32780e7c-aac0-5688-a09b-948445ba520b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159312} {:answer/id #uuid "4b0c73b3-1f00-5eb0-b4a8-0954c2dec4c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159313}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186159310} :answer-field/id #uuid "2914e011-6467-5eb9-b672-4230ceda87a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a22cb81e-00a5-5c3a-9983-3ce07b82cf44" :question/math-academy-id "q-137105" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ shown above. If the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $9a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 16 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}72 & -36 \\\\ -36 & 18\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}72 & -36 & 20 \\\\ -36 & 18 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}72 & -36 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{18}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{18}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n9a+\\frac{b}{c}=9\\cdot \\frac{5}{18}+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{1}=3.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "80b401f0-8dc4-5c1c-a4fc-3616a5846a72" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443612}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443611 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf25468f-d230-53f1-a94f-8f18037a8dfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363400} {:answer/id #uuid "24438d18-0c67-56f4-8912-fe1ead369af3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363401} {:answer/id #uuid "4bea5562-78d7-5083-8d55-27b52c42ffd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363402} {:answer/id #uuid "1245d519-bb23-5605-8a30-d545c6f84f82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363403} {:answer/id #uuid "ca976c63-1784-5536-a5ee-be049617b3de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363404}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363401} :answer-field/id #uuid "b10893fa-4f68-5f42-a725-ae358c74185c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2664fcbd-5b65-5a40-b14f-fb7042b6173a" :question/math-academy-id "q-158373" :question/problem "Find the solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {3}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 9 & -9 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}22 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the matrix on the left-hand side is invertible, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 22\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11\\cdot 22-8\\cdot 8}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 22\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{178}\\begin{bmatrix}178 \\\\ 178\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "87cab521-4e7d-503f-a838-7f04716620e6"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269151 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d1f5fd-cafb-568d-9657-f9184338194c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266187} {:answer/id #uuid "4ebabaa4-b41e-5183-994d-28b7279d1eef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "82" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266846} {:answer/id #uuid "d0905648-e8ad-5aec-9b17-ddbb11a74a74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "73" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268008} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e29d98-6dfb-5724-a307-5fe87ac6c792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "145" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268034} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b05ccb-64dd-53d9-9246-34046d164ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "109" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268331}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268331} :answer-field/id #uuid "a68dbddb-6995-52c9-8221-481a957ef459" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "47be74ee-5888-5dc1-a458-d8d8cd278e9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-137878" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & 20 \\\\ 20 & b\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ 29\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {3}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ -18 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -27\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}37 & 20 \\\\ 20 & 17\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}55 \\\\ 29\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=37+17+55=109.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d09a6f63-a2b3-5d2d-bc98-fffbf81bdb65" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443609}}] :activity/title "Weighted Least-Squares" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443600}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046