.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447161}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491271} :activity/id #uuid "c64c48fd-6421-5002-b8ac-ec2858dbbea6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049674 :topic/id #uuid "09e82368-7656-5709-b72f-b4db605d6406" :topic/math-academy-id 3013} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491224 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491194 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d27b381-2583-5b7a-9fca-f1b985469a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490564} {:answer/id #uuid "285d902e-e585-5afb-b7d0-a096327f6bdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490584} {:answer/id #uuid "720b4a42-214e-5e9a-9503-6877dcdc5e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490736} {:answer/id #uuid "88c5126e-12bf-54b2-b915-69e90cd55a29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490783} {:answer/id #uuid "cfaa20be-1127-571b-9b08-97a73a594545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490903}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490736} :answer-field/id #uuid "a928408c-e2bb-5ac3-8926-8be8d60e417f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bbae1413-e0da-5920-9a81-9f35b978911e" :question/math-academy-id "q-143422" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{50}$ is a sample of size $n=50$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =12$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=4,$ calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nSince the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=4$ and the sample size is $n=50,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\frac{4}{50} \\\\  & =0.08.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{0.08}≈0.28.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "145bbf22-1749-5090-aadc-e3554ab59101" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491278}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491233 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491183 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2340fb78-3bc0-52f2-8490-70b953ad6bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "101" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490575} {:answer/id #uuid "5361feae-c64c-5ad9-9e88-dc19f7cade4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "401" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490670} {:answer/id #uuid "78e50466-a068-54f3-a9e1-1405af2707ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "201" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490751} {:answer/id #uuid "96643632-e50d-5725-b254-61e0b31e5d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490816} {:answer/id #uuid "e63fe34a-b9f7-5470-aaa5-176d56673f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490951}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490575} :answer-field/id #uuid "2aee0077-edc2-5b86-802c-50ab5c7a8951" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9f4e58b8-0514-5aa4-9211-b553375a01af" :question/math-academy-id "q-143489" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ of $n$ coins is drawn from a population. The population has coins of two different denominations, $$1$ and $$0.50,$ distributed in the ratio $1:4.$ Determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than $$0.02.$" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the coins in the population have denominations $$1$ and $$0.50,$ and these are distributed in the ratio $1:4.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $1+4=5$ coins only with the following denominations:\n\n$$\n$1,\\,$0.5,\\,$0.5,\\,$0.5,\\,$0.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1+4\\cdot 0.5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =$0.6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}({1}^{2}+4\\cdot {0.5}^{2})-{0.6}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2)-0.36 \\\\  & =0.4-0.36 \\\\  & =0.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.04}=0.2,$ and we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{0.2}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $$0.02.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.2}{\\sqrt{n}} & <0.02 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{0.2}{0.02} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >10 \\\\ n & >{10}^{2} \\\\ n & >100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=101.$"} :step/id #uuid "46254631-f53c-514a-8d4a-8cae395482d2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491274}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491271 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162382 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "210567c7-e894-5b02-9ee8-c6893172df38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.18\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425735} {:answer/id #uuid "60c93555-b34b-575f-95b2-f38de1599831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.15\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425739} {:answer/id #uuid "ca20eb7d-1ae8-5c45-ab85-1bae5f9c5811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.32\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425747} {:answer/id #uuid "cce28df6-b4e2-5293-9860-139a95e35caa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.24\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425748} {:answer/id #uuid "cfed1752-e352-5a6b-9e2d-7dc6a36ff6ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.33\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425749}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425739} :answer-field/id #uuid "e23ca134-e512-533c-83e5-e06d13cf4c5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bcb58811-e371-5576-bf66-5ecfddbfc1fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-143202" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ of three nails is drawn from a population. The population has nails of two different lengths, $5\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{10}\\,\\text{cm},$ distributed in the ratio $4:1.$ Calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the nails in the population have lengths $5\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{10}\\,\\text{cm},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $4:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $4+1=5$ nails only with the following lengths:\n\n$$\n5\\,\\text{cm},\\,5\\,\\text{cm},\\,5\\,\\text{cm},\\,5\\,\\text{cm}\\,10\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\cdot 5+10) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(20+10) \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{5} \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{cm},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\cdot {5}^{2}+{10}^{2})-{6}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(200)-36 \\\\  & =40-36 \\\\  & =4\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}=\\frac{4}{3}\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{3}}≈1.15\\,\\text{cm}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "e09f4a70-dbb4-5510-9c9f-f62ce6e6b9da" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491224}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491274 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491123 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00419cc9-1483-5e06-b0b3-661e4e54899f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490512} {:answer/id #uuid "47723024-b4a1-584b-a829-85dceb9329c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490643} {:answer/id #uuid "80b0e3c0-0da7-58eb-94d5-d78a9853d5ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490770} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb60d40-47ca-586a-82af-614639d331fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490897} {:answer/id #uuid "e18c0630-b233-59ad-b582-a2188863f6f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490936}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490897} :answer-field/id #uuid "c686f557-e13f-5945-8efd-5e2a53507af7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "18073722-b26c-57f1-b864-bfe7c648363b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143495" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ of $n$ shirts is drawn from a population. The population has shirts of two different sizes, $8$ and $10,$ distributed in the ratio $1:3.$ Determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than $0.5.$" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the shirts in the population have sizes $8$ and $10,$ and these are distributed in the ratio $1:3.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $1+3=4$ shirts only with the following sizes:\n\n$$\n8,\\,10,\\,10,\\,10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(8+3\\cdot 10) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(8+30) \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{4} \\\\  & =9.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({8}^{2}+3\\cdot {10}^{2})-{9.5}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(364)-90.25 \\\\  & =91-90.25 \\\\  & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.75},$ and we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.5.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{n}} & <0.5 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{\\sqrt{0.75}}{0.5} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{{0.5}^{2}}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{0.25}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{3} \\\\ n & >3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=4.$"} :step/id #uuid "f623e816-8a9f-53dc-ac9b-5d259ddbb546"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491278 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491203 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eb4622f-90b6-5a96-a333-ac1ffe1c4ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "122" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490568} {:answer/id #uuid "7156e35a-a0cd-5c4a-9cfe-86803b267024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490734} {:answer/id #uuid "cad96efa-207b-59ed-9afb-6c27a870f1d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,305" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490895} {:answer/id #uuid "db47dba0-0807-5e27-ac1c-6bd558d9926c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490927} {:answer/id #uuid "dc1775bd-6de6-57e4-b281-5dc5531234f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490928}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490928} :answer-field/id #uuid "a38d7fe4-d3d9-5f07-8a46-a68180a20afb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "df86b42c-6e72-556f-b425-3d68c35d4d8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143440" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =24$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$  determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than $0.5.$" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nSince the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{1}=1,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.5.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} & <0.5 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{1}{0.5} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >2 \\\\ n & >{2}^{2} \\\\ n & >4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=5.$"} :step/id #uuid "faa8d334-a6c2-5d81-9e07-13cab6f2da50" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491233}}] :activity/title "Variance of Sample Means" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490511}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167900} :activity/id #uuid "eed4b5fc-95ba-5979-9241-b92cdec87053" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049830 :topic/id #uuid "1725b401-08f0-5bd3-9105-3249e65bebf9" :topic/math-academy-id 3211} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167900 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168037 :tutorial/content "In a homogeneous boundary value problem (BVP) that depends on a parameter $\\lambda$, most values of $\\lambda$ lead only to the trivial solution $y\\equiv 0$.  \n\nThe special values of $\\lambda$ that produce *nontrivial* solutions are called **eigenvalues**, and the corresponding nontrivial solutions are called **eigenfunctions**.\n\nFor example, consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\ny'' - 18\\lambda y' + 81\\lambda^2y  = 0, \\quad y(0) = 0, \\quad 4y(3) + 5y'(3) = 0, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\quad \\lambda \\in\\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nLet's find the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. \n\nThe method for solving the differential equation depends on whether $\\lambda = 0$ or $\\lambda \\neq 0.$ To find the eigenvalues, we must therefore investigate these two possibilities as separate cases.\n\nOn the next slide, we will analyze the case where $\\lambda = 0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1378993d-8bac-50d2-b1ea-4f7c3a1f5ec2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14646 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "887046eb-da38-509e-bc00-d45adf4aa497" :step/math-academy-id 592744 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167959}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167901 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166939 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166566 :question/id #uuid "877e1518-96ed-5b31-9e05-22990ce1cb72" :question/math-academy-id "e-21808"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba4d320f-517e-5dc6-9c6d-02d1c2488472" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050906}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522787}] :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Eigenfunctions"} :step/id #uuid "4251a9bf-5cd3-5cfc-ae56-c1aa466b5998" :step/math-academy-id 592745 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167903}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166940 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166565 :question/id #uuid "738bd502-dec6-589b-b198-5040fe7e3906" :question/math-academy-id "e-21806"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "472cbb79-e22b-50d2-a997-93130ff66c22" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049937}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522793}] :knowledge-point/title "Trigonometric Eigenfunctions"} :step/id #uuid "dc1ec685-f38d-51a2-9240-4f3a1f1346e1" :step/math-academy-id 592748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168038 :tutorial/content "When the auxiliary equation has purely imaginary roots, the solutions are trigonometric, and the boundary conditions usually force $\\lambda$ to take on a discrete set of values.\nSo, we again look for values of $\\lambda$ that allow a nontrivial solution.\n\nWe will now find the **eigenvalues** and **eigenfunctions** for the boundary value problem:\n\n$$\ny'' + 9\\lambda^2 y = 0, \\qquad y'(0) = 0, \\quad y(4) = 0, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq 4, \\quad \\lambda \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nWe consider two cases based on the value of $\\lambda,$ since they correspond to different forms of the general solution.\n\n**Case 1: $\\lambda = 0$**\n\nSetting $\\lambda = 0$ gives the equation $y'' = 0.$\nIntegrating this equation twice gives the general solution:\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B.\n$$\n\nTo apply the boundary conditions, we first find the derivative, $y' = A.$\n\n- Applying $y'(0) = 0$ gives $A = 0.$ The solution becomes $y = B.$\n\n- Applying $y(4) = 0$ then gives $B = 0.$\n\nThis yields the trivial solution $y \\equiv 0.$ Since an eigenfunction must be nontrivial, $\\lambda = 0$ is *not* an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2: $\\lambda > 0$**\n\nThe auxiliary equation is $m^2 + 9\\lambda^2 = 0,$ which has roots $m = \\pm 3i\\lambda.$\nThe general solution is therefore\n\n$$\ny = A\\cos (3\\lambda x) + B\\sin (3\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nNow we apply the boundary conditions. First, we find the derivative:\n\n$$\ny' = -3A\\lambda\\sin(3\\lambda x) + 3B\\lambda\\cos(3\\lambda x).\n$$\n\n- Applying the condition $y'(0) = 0$ yields:\n\n$$\n-3A\\lambda\\sin(0) + 3B\\lambda\\cos(0) = 0 \\quad\\implies\\quad 3B\\lambda = 0.\n$$\n\nSince we are in the case where $\\lambda > 0,$ this requires $B = 0.$ The solution simplifies to:\n\n$$\ny = A\\cos (3\\lambda x).\n$$\n\n- Next, applying the condition $y(4) = 0$ yields:\n \n\n$$\nA\\cos (12\\lambda) = 0.\n$$\n\n For a nontrivial solution, we must have $A \\neq 0,$ which means we require\n \n\n$$\n\\cos (12\\lambda) = 0.\n$$\n\n This condition is met when $12\\lambda$ is an odd multiple of $\\pi/2:$\n\n$$\n12\\lambda = \\dfrac{(2n+1)\\pi}{2}, \\quad \\text{for } n = 0, 1, 2, \\dots\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\lambda$ gives the set of **eigenvalues**:\n\n$$\n\\lambda_n = \\dfrac{(2n+1)\\pi}{24}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding **eigenfunctions** are \n\n$$\ny_n(x) = A_n \\cos(3\\lambda_n x) , \\quad A_n \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2e52b01a-4f53-5d27-9984-dd731308d87d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14650 :tutorial/title "Trigonometric Eigenfunctions"} :step/id #uuid "3e7ba88a-596c-53db-b70b-6b89ee975318" :step/math-academy-id 592751 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167902}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167958 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168055 :tutorial/content "Next, we consider the case $\\lambda \\neq 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions of \n\n$$\ny'' - 18\\lambda y' + 81\\lambda^2y  = 0,\n$$\n\nfor $\\lambda \\neq 0,$ we set $y = e^{m x}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-18\\lambda m+81{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-9\\lambda )(m-9\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives a repeated root $m = 9\\lambda.$ The general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{9\\lambda x} + Bxe^{9\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nNow we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Applying the first boundary condition, $y(0) = 0,$ yields\n\n$$\ny(0) = Ae^{0} + B(0)e^{0} = 0 \\quad \\implies \\quad A = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution simplifies to $y = Bxe^{9\\lambda x}.$ Its derivative is\n\n$$\ny' = Be^{9\\lambda x} + 9B\\lambda xe^{9\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\n- Applying the second boundary condition, $4y(3) + 5y'(3) = 0,$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(3B{e}^{27\\lambda })+5(B{e}^{27\\lambda }+27B\\lambda {e}^{27\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ 12B{e}^{27\\lambda }+5B{e}^{27\\lambda }+135B\\lambda {e}^{27\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{27\\lambda }(17+135\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a nontrivial solution (an eigenfunction), we require $B\\neq 0.$ Since $e^{27\\lambda}$ is never zero, we must have\n\n$$\n17+135\\lambda = 0 \\quad \\implies \\quad \\lambda = -\\dfrac{17}{135}.\n$$\n\nThis is our **eigenvalue**. The corresponding **eigenfunction** is\n\n$$\ny(x) = Bxe^{9(-17/135) x} = Bxe^{-17x/15}.\n$$\n\nNote that any nonzero constant multiple of this function is also an eigenfunction." :tutorial/id #uuid "f4d9fb35-7991-5b49-bfd6-c82ad2ef1c69" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14892 :tutorial/title "Non-Trivial Solution of the Eigenvalue Problem"} :step/id #uuid "81f0071e-ac02-5b8f-ba91-88209136e903" :step/math-academy-id 608067 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167901}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167959 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168056 :tutorial/content "We begin by considering  the case $\\lambda = 0.$\n\nSetting $\\lambda = 0$ in the original equation, $y'' - 18\\lambda y' + 81\\lambda^2y  = 0,$ gives a much simpler equation:\n\n$$\ny'' = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation twice with respect to $x$ gives the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B.\n$$\n\nNow we apply the boundary conditions. \n\n- The first condition, $y(0) = 0,$ implies:\n\n$$\ny(0) = A(0) + B = 0 \\quad \\implies \\quad B=0.\n$$\n\nSo the solution must be of the form $y = Ax.$\n\n- For the second condition, we first find the derivative, $y' = A.$ Applying the condition $4y(3) + 5y'(3) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(A\\cdot 3)+5(A) & =0 \\\\ 12A+5A & =0 \\\\ 17A & =0\\,\\,⟹\\,\\,A=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both $A=0$ and $B=0,$ we are left with the trivial solution $y \\equiv 0.$ By definition, an eigenfunction must be a *nontrivial* solution.\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda = 0$ is *not* an eigenvalue. \n\nNext, we will check the case where $\\lambda \\neq 0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b3458843-3f77-548b-b991-69c3d163d75d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14893 :tutorial/title "Trivial Solution of the Eigenvalue Problem"} :step/id #uuid "d95ffe14-fb14-518a-ac26-6970a9d57acf" :step/math-academy-id 608068 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167958}}] :activity/title "Eigenvalues and Eigenfunctions of Homogeneous BVPs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166995}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418250} :activity/id #uuid "eea1e25f-6efb-5cef-9851-420bd75de37e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049830 :topic/id #uuid "1725b401-08f0-5bd3-9105-3249e65bebf9" :topic/math-academy-id 3211} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418250 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365858 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ee92df8-a96b-5333-ae8d-b489df88c366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365860} {:answer/id #uuid "6f3b7016-4934-590e-b25b-b91ebf2125b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{5}$ and $\\frac{1}{10}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365861} {:answer/id #uuid "acc5b921-33a3-53bb-931e-2dc15cff77d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{10}$ and $\\frac{1}{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365862} {:answer/id #uuid "3b8a4734-03dd-59ba-8bf2-3aa5143e433c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365863}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365861} :answer-field/id #uuid "76d0668d-3ca7-569b-925d-2e956888c08a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365859} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f73ef24-52a4-5a30-9cf7-ddacd6a35251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365865} {:answer/id #uuid "7a1b1386-fefe-522a-9724-c7282fdf6830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{x}$ and $C{e}^{-x/2}(x+4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365866} {:answer/id #uuid "e9883a8c-7a90-5bdd-a094-5cb18a2dfc22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x+\\frac{3}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365867} {:answer/id #uuid "8e3aea8a-e9e7-5796-bf9a-ff68da034ea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365868}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365865} :answer-field/id #uuid "4984fab2-6b76-5c85-a24b-7e17e049b22f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c6e87982-ab6f-5e96-94e7-996ae7f3b605" :question/math-academy-id "q-338845" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-10\\lambda {y}^{′}+25{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-10\\lambda m+25{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-5\\lambda )(m-5\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=5\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{5\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{5\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{5\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5A\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda x}+B{e}^{5\\lambda x}+5B\\lambda x{e}^{5\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5A\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda }+B{e}^{5\\lambda }+5B\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 5A\\lambda +B+5B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{10\\lambda }+2B{e}^{10\\lambda }+2(5A\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda }+B{e}^{10\\lambda }+10B\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+2(5A\\lambda +B+10B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+10A\\lambda +2B+20B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )A+(4+20\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )A+4(1+5\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B &=0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )A+4(1+5\\lambda )B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}5\\lambda  & 1+5\\lambda  \\\\ 1+10\\lambda  & 4(1+5\\lambda )\\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\lambda \\cdot 4(1+5\\lambda )-(1+5\\lambda )(1+10\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1+5\\lambda )[20\\lambda -(1+10\\lambda )] & =0 \\\\ (1+5\\lambda )[20\\lambda -1-10\\lambda ] & =0 \\\\ (1+5\\lambda )(10\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{10}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+(1-1)B=0\n$$\n\n \nwhich gives $A=0,$ and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{10},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{3}{2}B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-3B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-3B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-3) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $Bx{e}^{-x}\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-3).$"} :step/id #uuid "10ec5288-96c7-54f4-a406-4dc3fa372454" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418254}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418251 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "333487d5-2b4f-53f7-a1f4-c935ce9920e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413422} {:answer/id #uuid "3a127422-744b-580f-8766-eedd4918927a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413424} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8a953c-29a1-5c31-b0ad-482482bf1033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413426} {:answer/id #uuid "9863903c-b7b0-5c31-8d4c-bcb8da7c8123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413430}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413424} :answer-field/id #uuid "314046e0-b3cb-5409-b06d-bb0e8f9f90c3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413435} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e54c974-6252-5708-b98d-d9bcad440480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413419} {:answer/id #uuid "74d7d903-23a2-5c49-b052-79f08cba23e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413428} {:answer/id #uuid "a7739e90-7698-5078-912c-cda3c5ade6a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413432} {:answer/id #uuid "f8cb5d6d-8680-580b-b06c-9309df73f9bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413434}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413428} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c50717b-94b2-5e1c-9cdd-e60b58c14e94" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "827b7203-4bc8-5ef4-ada5-12343dfe7439" :question/math-academy-id "q-338917" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9\\lambda y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(3)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(3)=0$ yields $A=0.$ Thus, we have a nontrivial solution, so $\\lambda =0$ is a parameter corresponding to eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+9\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 3\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+3B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-3A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+3B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $B=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(3)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-3A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9\\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n9\\sqrt{\\lambda }=n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{81}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "35485800-4576-505b-b01c-a3a46dd2634c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418252}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418252 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391720 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d299aab6-7d45-5daf-aeed-dbcaf20eb01f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391722} {:answer/id #uuid "50cf2632-5224-53c3-9780-700b593c95e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391723} {:answer/id #uuid "dad65025-c4f6-50bb-bfc0-81fdab4d27f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391724} {:answer/id #uuid "78ab4f16-e632-5d7c-a0ed-fca61eb6d86b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}\\pi }{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391725}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391724} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3e4d0a8-336d-51eb-9840-10ac5a3bc5ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391721} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "950d017d-4409-5a7d-93e6-82c04aebf61f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391727} {:answer/id #uuid "a76caf84-a8f6-5ed7-b166-b53efe8b63f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391728} {:answer/id #uuid "7e019b9a-dbcd-5aaa-9d4a-e9839bb29f61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391729} {:answer/id #uuid "3ae13067-ee42-5ad2-b168-11769b5a9ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391730}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391729} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf084e4c-8bde-52e0-8415-918ddbdb9317" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "55c03286-efa5-5f72-9411-1febab9b4b0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-338920" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16\\lambda y=0,\\,y(0)=y(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=y(\\pi )=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+16\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 4\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(\\pi )=0$ yields\n\n$$\nB\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n4\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda }=n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{16}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "742e6a8d-8e75-5c43-a20e-2b9e2df49b4e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418253 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391695 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80717e13-aa04-534e-b7cb-b9dd198b1171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391697} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9a1003-518c-5978-82a3-b12124ff2cae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391698} {:answer/id #uuid "cb175292-1d19-53ba-a268-6e1a4ae2b1d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391699} {:answer/id #uuid "ab342eb6-9f44-5413-9868-ba9ddfbb1